`,o.jsx(t.p,{children:"Any reasonably sized internal engagement stops being a single box problem very quickly. By the time you have rooted the first jump host you usually have three or four sets of credentials, a couple of SSH keys, a domain user, and a handful of half explored services. The mental model that fits this is a graph, not a list, because what matters in practice is the edge between two nodes: which credential let you go from one host to the next."}),`
`,o.jsx(t.p,{children:"This is exactly the situation you find yourself in throughout the OSEP labs, the challenge networks, and the 48 hour exam. I started using the workflow below while preparing for OSEP and it carried straight into client work. I will not be sharing any course material here, the examples are deliberately generic, but the shape of the problem is the same."}),`
`,o.jsx(t.p,{children:"Obsidian gives you that graph almost for free. Every wikilink in a note becomes an edge, and the Graph plugin that ships with Obsidian renders the whole vault as a force directed diagram you can pan, zoom, and filter. There is no separate database, no Neo4j, no API tokens. The vault is just a folder of markdown."}),`
`,o.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A ready to use template that follows the conventions below is published at ",o.jsx(t.a,{href:"https://github.com/pr0f94/obsidian-lateral-movement-template",children:"github.com/pr0f94/obsidian-lateral-movement-template"}),". Clone it, point Obsidian at the ",o.jsx(t.code,{children:"Engagement/"})," folder, and you have a working vault with a worked example chain already in place."]}),`
`,o.jsx(t.p,{children:o.jsx(t.img,{src:"images/graph-view.png",alt:"Obsidian Graph view of the example lateral movement chain, with Kali in blue and rooted identities in red"})}),`
